opus-4-8\"}}\n{\"type\":\"message_end\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"I'm going to look at the available skills and get started.\",\"thinkingSignature\":\"reasoning_content\"},{\"type\":\"toolCall\",\"id\":\"toolu_vrtx_01JTFf2v6FtqXaqAXswSSCcp\",\"name\":\"read\",\"arguments\":{\"path\":\".pi/skills/test-list/SKILL.md\"}},{\"type\":\"toolCall\",\"id\":\"toolu_vrtx_01VKpfdqKcPqLDytYxVbX2vQ\",\"name\":\"bash\",\"arguments\":{\"command\":\"ls -la src; echo \\\"---prev run log tail---\\\"; tail -20 run.log\"}}],\"api\":\"openai-completions\",\"provider\":\"requesty\",\"model\":\"vertex/claude-opus-4-8@eu\",\"usage\":{\"input\":10321,\"output\":143,\"cacheRead\":0,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"reasoning\":0,\"totalTokens\":10464,\"cost\":{\"input\":0,\"output\":0,\"cacheRead\":0,\"cacheWrite\":0,\"total\":0}},\"stopReason\":\"toolUse\",\"timestamp\":1784958742106,\"responseId\":\"rqsty-cmpl-2fec0631-f0bd-4497-a533-8c9c57672bfc\",\"responseModel\":\"claude-opus-4-8\"}}\n{\"type\":\"tool_execution_start\",\"toolCallId\":\"toolu_vrtx_01JTFf2v6FtqXaqAXswSSCcp\",\"toolName\":\"read\",\"args\":{\"path\":\".pi/skills/test-list/SKILL.md\"}}\n{\"type\":\"tool_execution_start\",\"toolCallId\":\"toolu_vrtx_01VKpfdqKcPqLDytYxVbX2vQ\",\"toolName\":\"bash\",\"args\":{\"command\":\"ls -la src; echo \\\"---prev run log tail---\\\"; tail -20 run.log\"}}\n{\"type\":\"tool_execution_update\",\"toolCallId\":\"toolu_vrtx_01VKpfdqKcPqLDytYxVbX2vQ\",\"toolName\":\"bash\",\"args\":{\"command\":\"ls -la src; echo \\\"---prev run log tail---\\\"; tail -20 run.log\"},\"partialResult\":{\"content\":[]}}\n{\"type\":\"tool_execution_end\",\"toolCallId\":\"toolu_vrtx_01JTFf2v6FtqXaqAXswSSCcp\",\"toolName\":\"read\",\"result\":{\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"---\\nname: test-list\\ndescription: TDD Test List Phase - Create a comprehensive test list covering every example and rule from the specification\\n---\\n\\n# TDD Test List Phase\\n\\nYou are now in the **Test List Phase** of TDD. Follow these instructions to create a comprehensive test list.\\n\\n## Your Mission\\n\\nCreate a test list using `it.todo()` that covers **every rule and every example** from the specification:\\n1. Read the specification (`prompt.md`) thoroughly -- every rule, every example, every clarifying question (?)\\n2. Turn each example into at least one `it.todo()` test case\\n3. Order tests from simplest to most complex\\n4. Use `it.todo()` only -- NO executable tests yet\\n\\n## Context: $ARGUMENTS\\n\\n## Process\\n\\n### Step 1: Understand the Feature\\nRead the complete specification. Pay special attention to integration examples and clarifying\\nquestions (marked with ?) -- these disambiguate rules that may seem open to interpretation in isolation.\\n- What are all the operations the system must support?\\n- What rules govern each operation?\\n- Which examples in the spec illustrate these rules?\\n\\n### Step 2: Identify Test Cases from the Spec\\nWalk through the specification section by section. For each rule and each example:\\n- Create a test case that verifies the described behavior\\n- Include the **expected values from the spec** in the test description\\n- If a clarifying question (?) resolves an ambiguity, create a test for the clarified interpretation\\n- If the spec uses an example-mapping format (rules, examples, questions), every listed example must have a corresponding test\\n\\n### Step 3: Order Tests (Simple -> Complex)\\nArrange tests in increasing complexity:\\n1. Simplest case (often empty/zero/single item)\\n2. Individual rules in isolation\\n3. Rules with modifiers\\n4. Combinations of multiple rules\\n5. Multi-step scenarios (e.g., operations that reference earlier results)\\n\\n### Step 4: Write Test File\\nCreate the test file with `it.todo()` entries:\\n\\n```typescript\\nimport { describe, it, expect } from \\\"vitest\\\";\\nimport { functionName } from \\\"./implementation.js\\\";\\n\\ndescribe(\\\"Feature Name\\\", () => {\\n  it.todo(\\\"should [behavior] -- [expected value from spec]\\\");\\n  it.todo(\\\"should [next behavior] -- [expected value from spec]\\\");\\n  // ... ordered simple -> complex, covering ALL spec examples\\n});\\n```\\n\\n### Step 5: Provide Summary\\n\\nAfter creating the test list, output:\\n\\n```\\nTest List Created:\\n**Feature**: [feature name]\\n**Test File**: [filename].spec.ts\\n**Tests**: [count]\\n\\n**Test Cases** (ordered simple -> complex):\\n1. [first test description]\\n2. [second test description]\\n3. [third test description]\\n...\\n\\n**Next Step**: Invoke `red` skill NOW, in this same turn, to activate the first test.\\n```\\n\\nDo not treat this summary as the end of your turn -- it is a checkpoint\\ninside one continuous run. Proceed straight into the Red phase (see\\n\\\"Completion -- DO NOT STOP HERE\\\" below).\\n\\n## Important Guidelines\\n\\n### DO\\n- Cover **every spec example** with at least one test\\n- Cover **every operation** described in the spec\\n- Give **every clarifying question (?)** a corresponding test\\n- Order tests **simple -> complex**\\n- Use `it.todo()` for all tests\\n- Include **expected values** in descriptions\\n- Keep tests **independent**\\n- One behavior per test\\n\\n### DON'T\\n- Write executable tests (use `it.todo()`)\\n- Think about implementation instead of behavior\\n- Miss an entire operation described in the spec\\n- Order randomly\\n\\n## Completion -- DO NOT STOP HERE\\n\\nThe test list is only a plan. Writing it is **not** a stopping point.\\nThis is a single autonomous run: the Test List Phase and the first Red\\nPhase happen in **one continuous turn**, with no pause and no handoff\\nbetween them.\\n\\nAfter printing the summary above, you MUST, in the **same turn**, without\\nwaiting for any confirmation:\\n\\n1. Read `.pi/skills/red/SKILL.md`.\\n2. Activate the first `it.todo()` test.\\n3. Produce the `## Red` heading and the `Red Phase Complete:` prediction\\n   lines for that first test.\\n\\nThen continue Red -> Green -> Refactor for every test until all pass and\\nyou have written `experiment-done.txt` containing `DONE`.\\n\\nEmit this exact line to mark the boundary, then immediately keep working\\nin the same turn:\\n\\n```\\nTest List Phase Complete. Continuing now to the Red phase for test 1 (no stop).\\n```\\n\\nThe words after that line in your output MUST be the `## Red` heading for\\ntest 1. If your turn ends with the test list, the run has failed.\\n\"}]},\"isError\":false}\n{\"type\":\"tool_execution_update\",\"toolCallId\":\"toolu_vrtx_01VKpfdqKcPqLDytYxVbX2vQ\",\"toolName\":\"bash\",\"args\":{\"command\":\"ls -la src; echo \\\"---prev run log tail---\\\"; tail -20 run.log\"},\"partialResult\":{\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"total 8\\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 experimenter experimenter 4096 Jul 25 05:52 .\\ndrwxr-xr-x 5 experimenter experimenter 4096 Jul 25 05:52 ..\\n\"}],\"details\":{}}}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784958744731},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'll review the red and green skills to build out the test list.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"Now let me read the red and green skills, then create the test list."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_01SsZi8DydNj8jMDemhsvLSS","name":"read","arguments":{"path":".pi/skills/red/SKILL.md"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_015E1RYoqiygBnnTfiXFD6dk","name":"read","arguments":{"path":".pi/skills/green/SKILL.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"vertex/claude-opus-4-8@eu","usage":{"input":29292,"output":153,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":29445,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784958744732,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-020cb8fb-19c7-4856-b7f4-dbf42a674695","responseModel":"claude-opus-4-8"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_vrtx_01SsZi8DydNj8jMDemhsvLSS","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: red\ndescription: TDD Red Phase - Activate ONE test from the test list and make it fail with explicit predictions\n---\n\n# TDD Red Phase\n\nYou are now in the **Red Phase** of TDD. Follow these instructions to activate ONE test and make it fail.\n\n## Your Mission\n\n1. Activate exactly ONE test from the test list\n2. Make explicit predictions about how it will fail\n3. Verify the test fails for the right reason\n4. Maintain strict discipline - NO implementation during Red phase\n\n## Context: $ARGUMENTS\n\n## Red Phase Rules\n\n- **One test at a time**: Convert exactly ONE `it.todo()` to executable test\n- **All other tests remain as `it.todo()`**: Never have more than one failing test\n- **Two-stage failure**: First compilation error, then runtime/assertion error\n- **Make predictions**: Explicitly state expected failures before running tests\n- **No implementation**: Don't write code to make test pass yet\n\n## Process\n\n### Step 1: Activate One Test\n\nIdentify the next `it.todo()` and convert it to executable test code:\n\n```typescript\n// Convert from:\nit.todo(\"should return 0 for empty input\");\n\n// To:\nit(\"should return 0 for empty input\", () => {\n  expect(calculate(\"\")).toBe(0);\n});\n```\n\nLeave all other tests as `it.todo()`.\n\n### Step 2: Predict Compilation Error\n\nBefore running the test, state your prediction:\n\n```\nRed Phase - Compilation Error Prediction:\n- Test: \"should return 0 for empty input\"\n- Expected: Compilation error\n- Reason: Function `calculate` doesn't exist yet\n- Error: \"Cannot find name 'calculate'\"\n```\n\n### Step 3: Run Test - Verify Compilation Error\n\nRun `pnpm test` and verify:\n- Compilation error as predicted, OR\n- Prediction wrong -> STOP and explain discrepancy\n\n### Step 4: Create Empty Function\n\nCreate minimal function stub (no logic):\n\n```typescript\nexport const calculate = (input: string): number => {\n  return undefined as unknown as number; // Intentionally wrong\n};\n```\n\n### Step 5: Predict Runtime Error\n\nBefore running again, state your prediction:\n\n```\nRed Phase - Runtime Error Prediction:\n- Test: \"should return 0 for empty input\"\n- Expected: Runtime assertion error\n- Expected value: 0\n- Actual value: undefined\n- Diff:\n  Expected: 0\n  Received: undefined\n```\n\n### Step 6: Run Test - Verify Runtime Error\n\nRun `pnpm test` and verify:\n- Assertion error as predicted, OR\n- Prediction wrong -> STOP and explain discrepancy\n\n### Step 7: Report Completion\n\nYou MUST output the full Step 7 block verbatim with `Correct` or `Incorrect`\nchosen for each prediction. Do not abbreviate. Do not collapse the two\nprediction lines into one.\n\n**Why this format matters:** The block is mechanically parsed to compute\n`predictions_correct_rate`. The parser expects two lines matching\n`(- |Correct|Incorrect)` per cycle -- one for the compilation prediction,\none for the runtime prediction. Collapsing them into a single line, summarizing\nthem as \"both correct\", or skipping the block entirely drops the predictions\ncount for this cycle to zero. Format consistency here directly drives a metric\nthe experiment measures.\n\n```\nRed Phase Complete:\n**Test Activated**: \"should return 0 for empty input\"\n**Compilation Prediction**: Cannot find name 'calculate' Correct\n**Runtime Prediction**: Expected 0, received undefined Correct\n**Result**: Test fails as expected with assertion error\n\nProceeding to Green phase.\n```\n\n## Important Guidelines\n\n### DO\n- Activate exactly ONE test at a time\n- Make explicit predictions before running tests\n- Verify test fails for the right reason\n- Keep all other tests as `it.todo()`\n\n### DON'T\n- Activate multiple tests\n- Skip making predictions\n- Write implementation to make test pass\n- Continue if prediction fails without explanation\n\n## Prediction Failure Protocol\n\nIf your prediction was wrong:\n\n```\nPrediction Failed:\n- Predicted: [what you expected]\n- Actual: [what happened]\n- Discrepancy: [explanation]\n\nInvestigating the discrepancy before proceeding.\n```\n\n## Completion\n\nAfter completing Red phase, proceed to Green phase:\n\n```\nRed Phase Complete. Proceeding to Green phase.\n```\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784958747252},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_vrtx_015E1RYoqiygBnnTfiXFD6dk","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"---\nname: green\ndescription: TDD Green Phase - Implement minimal code to make the failing test pass\n---\n\n# TDD Green Phase\n\nYou are now in the **Green Phase** of TDD. Follow these instructions to make the failing test pass with MINIMAL code.\n\n## Why minimality matters\n\nThe Green Phase deliberately writes the smallest implementation that turns the active test green -- even hardcoded returns, even obviously-incomplete logic. This is not laziness or naivete:\n\n- It **exposes refactoring opportunities** in the next phase. When the implementation does exactly one thing, structural smells are visible. Over-implemented green code looks \"already clean\" and hides design problems.\n- It **prevents premature generalization**. The shape of the right abstraction emerges from the second and third test, not the first. Implementing for hypothetical future tests locks in a design before its constraints are known.\n- It **keeps the red-green-refactor cycle short**. Each cycle pays a cost in tokens and context; over-implementing in green collapses several future cycles into one large one and loses the per-test feedback loop.\n\n## Your Mission\n\n1. Implement the **minimal code** necessary to make the failing test pass\n2. Use the **simplest possible solution** (hardcoded values are acceptable)\n3. Avoid adding features for future tests\n4. Verify all tests pass\n5. NO optimization, NO refactoring yet\n\n## Context: $ARGUMENTS\n\n## Green Phase Rules\n\n- **Minimal code only**: Just enough to pass the current test\n- **Baby steps**: Make the smallest possible change\n- **No future features**: Don't implement what future tests might need\n- **Simple is better**: Hardcoded returns are perfectly fine\n- **Tests must pass**: Verify all tests are green\n- **No refactoring yet**: Save improvements for Refactor phase\n\n## Process\n\n### Step 1: Analyze the Failing Test\n\nUnderstand what the test expects:\n- What input does the test provide?\n- What output does it expect?\n- What is the **simplest** way to produce that output?\n\n### Step 2: Write Minimal Implementation\n\nImplement **only what's needed** to make the current test pass:\n\n```typescript\n// For first test \"should return 0 for empty input\":\nexport const calculate = (input: string): number => {\n  return 0; // Minimal - just make the test pass\n};\n\n// For second test \"should return number for single input\":\nexport const calculate = (input: string): number => {\n  if (input === \"\") return 0;\n  return parseInt(input); // Still simple\n};\n\n// For third test \"should add two numbers\":\nexport const calculate = (input: string): number => {\n  if (input === \"\") return 0;\n  const numbers = input.split(\",\");\n  if (numbers.length === 1) return parseInt(numbers[0]);\n  return parseInt(numbers[0]) + parseInt(numbers[1]); // Only now add logic\n};\n```\n\n### Step 3: Run Tests\n\nRun `pnpm test` and verify:\n- Current test now passes\n- All previous tests still pass\n\n### Step 4: Verify No Over-Implementation\n\nCheck yourself:\n- Did I implement features for future tests? -> Remove them\n- Did I add logic not demanded by current test? -> Remove it\n- Did I optimize prematurely? -> Simplify\n- Did I refactor existing code? -> Revert, save for Refactor phase\n\n### Step 5: Report Completion\n\n```\nGreen Phase Complete:\n**Implementation**: [brief description of what was added]\n**Result**: All tests now pass ([X] passing)\n**Approach**: [explain why this is minimal]\n\nProceeding to Refactor phase.\n```\n\n## Minimal Implementation Strategies\n\n### Hardcoded Returns (Preferred for Early Tests)\n```typescript\n// Test: \"should return 0 for empty input\"\nreturn 0; // Perfect - minimal\n```\n\n### Simple Conditionals (When Multiple Tests)\n```typescript\n// Test: \"should return number for single input\"\nif (input === \"\") return 0;\nreturn parseInt(input); // Still simple\n```\n\n### Generalization (Only When Forced)\n```typescript\n// Test: \"should add multiple numbers\" - NOW generalize\nreturn input.split(\",\").reduce((sum, n) => sum + parseInt(n), 0);\n```\n\n## Important Guidelines\n\n### DO\n- Write minimal code to make test pass\n- Use hardcoded values when appropriate\n- Take baby steps\n- Verify all tests pass\n\n### DON'T\n- Implement beyond what tests demand\n- Add features for future tests\n- Optimize prematurely\n- Refactor during Green phase\n\n## Psychological Resistance\n\nYou will feel resistance:\n- **\"This is too simple\"** -> That's correct! Minimal is the way\n- **\"Hardcoded values feel wrong\"** -> They're exactly right for early tests\n- **\"I should implement ahead\"** -> Resist this strongly\n- **\"This is inefficient\"** -> Actually accelerates development\n\nTrust the process. Simple steps compound into elegant solutions.\n\n## Completion\n\nAfter completing Green phase, proceed to Refactor phase:\n\n```\nGreen Phase Complete. Proceeding to Refactor phase.\n```\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784958747252},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm setting up the test cases to drive the development process.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"text","text":"All skills read. Now I'll create the test list.\n\n## Test List\n\nFollowing the test-list skill, I'll cover every rule and example from the spec, ordered simple → complex."},{"type":"toolCall","id":"toolu_vrtx_01P24ssx3bJUVoRy46if7aY8","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"src/game-of-life.spec.ts","content":"import { describe, it, expect } from \"vitest\";\nimport { nextGeneration } from \"./game-of-life.js\";\n\ndescribe(\"Game of Life - Next Generation\", () => {\n  // Simplest cases\n  it.todo(\"returns an empty array for an empty grid\");\n  it.todo(\"a single live cell dies (0 neighbors) -- [(0,0)] -> []\");\n\n  // Rule 1 - Underpopulation\n  it.todo(\"Rule 1: two adjacent live cells both die (1 neighbor each) -- [(0,1),(1,1)] -> []\");\n\n  // Rule 2 - Survival\n  it.todo(\"Rule 2: a live cell with 2 or 3 neighbors survives (blinker center (1,1) survives)\");\n\n  // Rule 3 - Overpopulation\n  it.todo(\"Rule 3: a live cell with more than 3 neighbors dies (center (1,1) of full 3x3 dies)\");\n\n  // Rule 4 - Reproduction\n  it.todo(\"Rule 4: a dead cell with exactly 3 neighbors becomes alive -- (1,1) born\");\n\n  // Pattern examples\n  it.todo(\"Blinker oscillates: [(0,0),(0,1),(0,2)] -> [(-1,1),(0,1),(1,1)]\");\n  it.todo(\"Block still life is unchanged: [(0,0),(1,0),(0,1),(1,1)] stays the same\");\n});\n"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"requesty","model":"vertex/claude-opus-4-8@eu","usage":{"input":24765,"output":627,"cacheRead":7940,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":0,"totalTokens":33332,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1784958747253,"responseId":"rqsty-cmpl-72470c85-833e-4d69-8708-f66194c2ad24","responseModel":"claude-opus-4-8"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"toolu_vrtx_01P24ssx3bJUVoRy46if7aY8","toolName":"write","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Successfully wrote 977 bytes to src/game-of-life.spec.ts"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1784958754913},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Test List Created:\n**Feature**: Game of Life - Next Generation\n**Test File**: src/game-of-life.spec.ts\n**Tests**: 9\n\n**Test Cases** (ordered simple → complex):\n1. returns empty array for empty grid\n2. single live cell dies (0 neighbors)\n3. Rule 1: underpopulation — two adjacent cells die\n4. Rule 2: survival — blinker center survives\n5. 